    ('homelessness', 3, 'Reyes joined the unanimous June 26, 2023 vote adopting Tualatin''s "time, place, and manner" camping ordinance, which prohibits camping in parks, parking lots, within 20 feet of buildings and 500 feet of freeways and schools, while designating a single city-owned parcel next to the police station (capacity about 50 tents) where camping is allowed 7 p.m. to 7 a.m. She explained her vote as deliberate incrementalism tied to the designated alternative site: "I''m in favor of starting slowly, then moving and progressing. I''m in favor of the police station lot." The ordinance was expressly written to comply with the Oregon-law standard that people cannot be punished for sleeping outside when no alternative shelter is available, with Washington County building a shelter-bed availability system to guide enforcement. Supporting regulated enforcement conditioned on an available alternative location matches the enforcement-only-when-shelter-is-available chair, not a blanket criminal ban or an unrestricted right to camp in public spaces.', ARRAY['https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/new-ordinance-limits-hours-and-locations-for-houseless-camping/', 'https://www.tualatinoregon.gov/administration/camping-regulations']::text[]),
    ('homelessness-response', 3, 'Reyes''s documented approach pairs reasonable public-space rules with services and housing access: she voted for the unanimous June 26, 2023 camping ordinance that restricts camping citywide while providing a designated overnight site next to the police station, saying "I''m in favor of starting slowly, then moving and progressing. I''m in favor of the police station lot." At the Sept 30, 2024 "Barriers and Solutions to Housing" community discussion she focused on the services side, asking regional housing experts how people stuck on long waiting lists could reach affordable apartments faster and calling it "a tough situation." The ordinance she supported operates within a county shelter-bed availability framework rather than as punitive-first enforcement. That combination — pushing housing and service connections for unhoused residents alongside enforceable time-place-manner rules — matches the outreach-shelter-and-services-with-reasonable-rules chair rather than the enforcement-primary or no-enforcement housing-first chairs.', ARRAY['https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/new-ordinance-limits-hours-and-locations-for-houseless-camping/', 'https://tualatinlife.com/local-news/officials-discuss-solutions-for-tualatins-affordable-housing-crisis/', 'https://www.tualatinoregon.gov/administration/camping-regulations']::text[]),
    ('civil-rights', 2, 'Reyes has a sustained record of institutionalizing inclusion work in Tualatin government: she came to the council from the city''s volunteer Diversity Task Force (which she joined in 2016), became the first Latina ever elected to the Tualatin City Council in Nov 2018, and in February 2025 voted with the 6-1 majority — over Councilor Octavio Gonzalez''s dissent — to proclaim March 31 Transgender Day of Visibility, recognizing transgender and nonbinary residents'' contributions to the community. During her tenure the council also created the Inclusion, Diversity, Equity, and Access (IDEA) Advisory Committee (2023) to set and measure equity goals for city government. Her official city biography highlights her ongoing engagement with TuaLatinos and the Café con Pan Dulce Latino business network as core councilor activity. Taking affirmative public actions to advance inclusion for immigrant, Latino, and LGBTQ residents — over dissent when necessary — goes beyond merely maintaining existing law, matching the strengthen-protections chair, while nothing in her record supports institution-wide mandates or reparations.', ARRAY['https://tualatinlife.com/featured/tualatin-to-recognize-transgender-day-of-visibility/', 'https://tualatinoregon.gov/city-council/', 'https://tualatinlife.com/featured/meet-your-councilor-maria-a-reyes/']::text[]),
